  "awb---faq-data": "<div data-rt-embed-type='true'><div data-rt-embed-type='true'><script> window.awbFAQ = {\"heading\": \"Photo Contest Form FAQ\", \"items\": [{\"q\": \"What is a photo contest form?\", \"a\": \"A photo contest form is the online form entrants use to submit photos to a contest. It saves each upload as a numbered entry with the entrant's details, and the organizer judges from that one list instead of an inbox of attachments.\"}, {\"q\": \"How do I create a photo contest form?\", \"a\": \"Describe the contest in Emergent's <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-form-builder'>AI form builder</a>: the categories, the limit per entrant, and how judges score. For example: \\\"Build a photo contest form for my garden club with 3 categories and 2 photos per category.\\\" Emergent turns that sentence into the form and the table behind it, and the free tier covers building and publishing.\"}, {\"q\": \"How many photos should a photo contest form let each entrant submit?\", \"a\": \"Allow 2 or 3 per category for a club contest, then check the total against your judges' time. A club of 60 members entering 2 photos in each of 3 categories can send up to 360 photos. At 30 seconds per photo, that is 3 hours of judging for each judge. Lower the cap if that is too long.\"}, {\"q\": \"Should a photo contest form offer more than one category?\", \"a\": \"Yes, once your entrants shoot different subjects. Make the category a required pick from a fixed list. Every photo then lands in exactly one, and judges score one category at a time. For a contest without a photo, such as a recipe or essay contest, a plain <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-form-builder/contest-entry'>contest entry form</a> is the better fit.\"}, {\"q\": \"How do I hide entrant names on a photo contest form from judges?\", \"a\": \"Show judges an entry number in place of the name, then check what else gives the entrant away. A file named after its owner, or a title that mentions them, breaks the blind. Ask for each upload to be saved under its entry number, and keep the title as the only words judges read.\"}, {\"q\": \"When should a photo contest form stop taking entries?\", \"a\": \"Close it at a fixed date and time, and print the time zone next to it, such as 11:59 p.m. Eastern on March 31. Leave at least a week between the close and the results so judges are not rushed. In a form built on Emergent, the app emails your mailing list a reminder 2 days before the close.\"}, {\"q\": \"Should a talent search use a photo contest form?\", \"a\": \"No. A talent search judges the person, and a photo contest judges the picture. Use an <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-form-builder/audition'>audition form</a> instead: it records the performer, the role they want, and when they can come in, with headshots attached to the application.\"}, {\"q\": \"Can visitors vote on entries from a photo contest form?\", \"a\": \"Yes. Add a gallery page where each photo shows its entry number and a vote button, and allow one vote per email address for each photo. Keep the public votes and the judges' stars in separate columns to name both a judges' pick and a people's choice. Forms built on Emergent have no response caps, and a vote that spreads past your members keeps counting.\"}, {\"q\": \"Can one photo contest form run a new contest every month?\", \"a\": \"Yes. Keep one form and add the contest month to each entry. The limit per category then resets when the month changes. The entries live in a database you own: every member's past months stay in one table, and you can see who entered all season.\"}, {\"q\": \"Where should I post a photo contest form so people enter?\", \"a\": \"Give the contest its own page with the prize, the deadline, and last year's winning photos, and put the form on that page. If the prize is the main draw and the photo is only the way in, a <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-landing-page-builder/giveaway'>giveaway landing page</a> fits better.\"}]}; </script></div></div>"
 }
